  "caveats": [
    "rmse320 is measured against THIS renderer at renderScale 1.0, not a path-traced ground truth. It answers 'how far is this setting from the best the renderer does at this pose', not 'how physically right is it'.",
    "rmse320 is a global average and is blind to localized-but-important error. Museum GI is the clearest case: turning gi off costs 25.5 ms and only 0.41 rmse, because the museum's indirect bounce is a small fraction of total radiance at that pose — it is NOT a claim that GI does not matter there.",
    "For the opt-in LOOK features (volumetric, kmScattering) the rmse delta measures how much the picture changed on purpose, not error. They are excluded from the Pareto frontier for that reason.",
    "sharpRatio is mean |Laplacian| and counts noise as detail. It is trustworthy on the resolution axes (comparable noise levels) and meaningless on the taa axis, where taa-off reads 2.4-4.4x 'sharper' purely from aliasing and grain.",
    "stillNoise on the taa axis is not comparable either: TAA adds sub-pixel jitter every frame, so a perfectly converged TAA frame still differs from the last one. The taa=false arm is the no-TAA floor the repo's historical metric uses.",
    "gridEnergy is a single-bin DFT over a 128px probe window. Bins at period <=16px (8+ cycles) are reliable; the 32 and 64px bins have 4 and 2 cycles and are contaminated by scene curvature that a plane detrend does not remove. At those periods the amplified difference images (quality-campaign/zoom) are the primary evidence, not the number.",
    "Post-motion 'ghost' numbers include residual sampling noise as well as stale signal, so a noisier config can score worse without ghosting more. Compare the P95 residual (localized) alongside the mean.",
    "Every number is one GPU (RTX 3060), one driver (ANGLE OpenGL), one viewport (1280x720, pixelRatio 1), three.js r160. Feature costs are strongly scene-dependent by design — that is why the matrix is per scene."
  ]
}